okay today I'm going to talk to you  about acids and bases we're gonna see  what acids and bases actually are we're  gonna learn about the pH scale we're  gonna look at different indicators and  see what they tell us about different  substances so let's start off by looking  at this thing here which is called the  pH scale so the pH scale is a scale  which ranges from zero to 14 and  depending on where a substance is on  this scale tells us whether it's an acid  neutral or an alkaline so let's start  off by looking at acids so acids is  anything with a pH of less than 7 so all  of this section here of the pH scale are  all acids  now what acid actually is is it's a kind  of show of the concentration of H+ ions  hydrogen ions so the substance down here  let's say pH 1 will have an extra will  be a very strong acid something like  stomach acid or battery acid so this  means it has a very high concentration  of of hydrogen ions as we go closer to 7  we have a lower and lower concentration  of hydrogen ions so maybe here around 4  or 5 we actually it's still acidic  because it's still less than 7 but now  we have a weak acid instead when we get  to 7 pH 7 we describe these substances  as being neutral water is an example  pure water an example of a neutral  substance which means there's not a high  concentration of h+ ions and there's not  a high concentration of h minus ions  because let's have a look at the bases  so first of all I think it's important  there's two different phrases there are  two different key terms that we often  hear one is alkali and one is base so a  base is a substance which reacts with an  acid  to make salt and water that is the  definition there of a base all an alkali  is all an alkali is it's a base which is  soluble in water so they're similar but  please be aware of that subtle  difference now our bases or our alkalis  now anything with a pH above 7 so all of  these here are my alkalis now acids is  all about concentration of h+ ions  alkalis is all about concentration of Oh  H minus ions so it then behaves in a  really similar way that we saw from our  acids so right at this end we have a  very high concentration of our h minus  ions that means over here we have  extremely strong alkalis such as bleach  the closer we get to neutral the lower  and lower the concentration is of our H  minus ions so let's say around 9 or 8  here we have weak alkalis now the most  harmful substances to do with acids and  alkalis layer either end of the scale so  if I was to spill this on my hand a very  strong acid or a very strong alkali my  hand it could really damage me whereas  in the in the middle with neutral  substances that don't have a high  concentration of h+ ions or a high  concentration of our h minus ions  they're much safer to compare to our  strong acids or a strong alkalis so  what's this color scale all about so  there's different things called  indicators and what indicators do is  they change color to show us if a  substance is an acid neutral an alkaline  and the one that this scale is based on  here is an indicator called universal  indicator it's very useful because it  shows us if we added a few drops of  universal indicator to an unknown sub  it would change to one of these colors  to show the to show whether it was a  strong acid weak acid we'd be able to  find the pH based on the color change so  Universal indicator gives us a nice  change in spectrum of colors like this  there's also three other indicators that  we need to know about and what they look  like in each different condition so  first of all we have litmus in acidic  conditions it's red in neutral  conditions it's purple and in alkaline  conditions it is blue methyl Orange is a  different indicator against thee which  changes color to show us whether it's an  acid alcohol neutral in Civic conditions  that is also red in neutral conditions  and alkali conditions it's yellow now  the reason why the reason why I'm not  writing the word yellow and yellow is  just because it wouldn't show up for the  video now finally this really difficult  looking word phenolphthalein is another  indicator so phenolphthalein in acidic  conditions is colorless in neutral  conditions it's also colorless but in  alkaline conditions it have a color  change and it will appear pink so you  could use any of these different  indicators in order to determine whether  you have an acid or an alkali or a  neutral solution on your hands and  that's all we need to know about acids  and bases thank you
 

